}]$" "$\\lambda =4∓\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ -3∓\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186361933 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186361937 :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{35}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{35}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186361964 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 & -11 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -11 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186361969 :answer/value "$\\lambda =1∓2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1∓2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" "$\\lambda =1∓2\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1∓2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186361971 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186362212 :question/problem "Consider the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ given by\n\n$$\nC={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T:V\\to {R}^{2}$ relative to these bases if \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{3})=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Consider the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ given by\n\n$$\nC={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T:V\\to {R}^{2}$ relative to these bases if \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,T({b}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,T({b}_{3})=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362212 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2,3.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -8 & 2 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 7 & -10\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 10 & -20 & 27 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 7 & -10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}10 & -20 & 27 \\\\ -4 & 7 & -10\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2,3.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -8 & 2 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 7 & -10\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 10 & -20 & 27 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 7 & -10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -20 & 27 \\\\ -4 & 7 & -10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362213 :question/problem "Consider the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ given by\n\n$$\nC=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T:V\\to {R}^{2}$ relative to these bases if \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{3})=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Consider the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ given by\n\n$$\nC=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T:V\\to {R}^{2}$ relative to these bases if \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,T({b}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,T({b}_{3})=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362213 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2,3.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -8 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2,3.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -8 & 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362214 :question/problem "Consider the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ given by\n\n$$\nC={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T:V\\to {R}^{2}$ relative to these bases if \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Consider the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ given by\n\n$$\nC={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T:V\\to {R}^{2}$ relative to these bases if \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,T({b}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362214 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -11 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 12 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -11 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 12 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362215 :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -7 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W,$ respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:V\\to W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x\\in V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$" "$$\n[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -7 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W,$ respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:V\\to W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x\\in V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186362215 :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -7 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -7 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362216 :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W,$ respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:V\\to W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x\\in V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$" "$$\n[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W,$ respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:V\\to W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x\\in V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186362216 :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362217 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ (shown above) and a basis $C$ of ${R}^{3}.$\nThe matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to these bases, and the vector $x\\in {R}^{2},$ are also given. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$" "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ (shown above) and a basis $C$ of ${R}^{3}.$\nThe matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to these bases, and the vector $x\\in {R}^{2},$ are also given. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186362217 :question/worked-solution "We can find the coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $[x]_{B},$ the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B.$ So, let\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives us the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}=-4 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now row-reduce the corresponding augmented matrix using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can find the coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $[x]_{B},$ the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B.$ So, let\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}=-4 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now row-reduce the corresponding augmented matrix using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362240 :question/problem "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \n\nNormalize the  vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \n\nNormalize the  vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ under this inner product."] [:db/cas 17592186362240 :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨v,v⟩ & =1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)+2\\cdot (3\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =1+12+18 \\\\  & =31.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{31}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $v$, we divide $v$ by its norm $‖v‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{31}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{31}}{31}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨v,v⟩ & =1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)+2\\cdot (3\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =1+12+18 \\\\  & =31.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{31}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $v$, we divide $v$ by its norm $‖v‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{31}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{31}}{31}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362241 :question/problem "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩=3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \n\nNormalize the  vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩=3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \n\nNormalize the  vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ under this inner product."] [:db/cas 17592186362241 :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨v,v⟩ & =3\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot (1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =12+1+3 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{16}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $v$, we divide $v$ by its norm $‖v‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨v,v⟩ & =3\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot (1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =12+1+3 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{16}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $v$, we divide $v$ by its norm $‖v‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362242 :question/problem "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩=2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \n\nNormalize the  vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩=2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \n\nNormalize the  vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ under this inner product."] [:db/cas 17592186362242 :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨v,v⟩ & =2\\cdot (3\\cdot 3)+2\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)+(-3)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =18+8+9 \\\\  & =35.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $v$, we divide $v$ by its norm $‖v‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{35}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨v,v⟩ & =2\\cdot (3\\cdot 3)+2\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)+(-3)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =18+8+9 \\\\  & =35.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $v$, we divide $v$ by its norm $‖v‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{35}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362258 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=2.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ find the principal axes of the curve." "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=2.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ find the principal axes of the curve."] [:db/cas 17592186362258 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=2\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=2\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186362259 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=3.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ find the principal axes of the curve." "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=3.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ find the principal axes of the curve."] [:db/cas 17592186362259 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=3\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-4)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=3\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lam