\\lambda }_{2}=-7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-7)I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{aligned}]+7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-7)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=3\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-7)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}+7\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-7)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186362261 :question/problem "$3[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]=$" "$3\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186362261 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each entry of the matrix by $3$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 4 & 3\\cdot (-5) \\\\ 3\\cdot (-2) & 3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & -15 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We multiply each entry of the matrix by $3$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 4 & 3\\cdot (-5) \\\\ 3\\cdot (-2) & 3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -15 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362340 :question/problem "Find the possible values of the constant $k$ given that the matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & k \\\\ 1 & k & -k \\\\ 5 & k & k\\end{aligned}]$ is singular.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $k=-3$\n- **II.** $k=0$\n- **III.** $k=3$" "Find the possible values of the constant $k$ given that the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & k \\\\ 1 & k & -k \\\\ 5 & k & k\\end{bmatrix}$ is singular.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $k=-3$\n- **II.** $k=0$\n- **III.** $k=3$"] [:db/cas 17592186362358 :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$"] [:db/cas 17592186362358 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -(-1) & -2\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=-2$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-1.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2+(-1)\\text{i}=-2-\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3+0\\text{i} \\\\ 5+(-2)\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2∓\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5∓2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -(-1) & -2\\end{bmatrix},$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=-2$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-1.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2+(-1)\\text{i}=-2-\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix},$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}3+0\\text{i} \\\\ 5+(-2)\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2∓\\text{i},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5∓2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362360 :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$"] [:db/cas 17592186362360 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -(-2) & 0\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-2.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =0+(-2)\\text{i}=-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}4+1\\text{i} \\\\ -3+(-1)\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4+\\text{i} \\\\ -3-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =∓2\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}4\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ -3∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -(-2) & 0\\end{bmatrix},$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-2.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =0+(-2)\\text{i}=-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix},$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}4+1\\text{i} \\\\ -3+(-1)\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4+\\text{i} \\\\ -3-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =∓2\\text{i},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ -3∓\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362361 :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n