only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second and third columns, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second and third columns, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ has both properties."] [:db/cas 17592186364525 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186364526 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186364527 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186364528 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186364529 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186364530 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$"] [:db/cas 17592186364530 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=8.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n\n$$\n{e}_{1}={v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we find ${e}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ by their respective norms, we obtain the first and second columns of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}_{1}}{‖{e}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{e}_{2}}{‖{e}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=8.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n\n$$\n{e}_{1}={v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand we find ${e}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ by their respective norms, we obtain the first and second columns of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}_{1}}{‖{e}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\frac{{e}_{2}}{‖{e}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186364532 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\end{aligned}]$" "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186364533 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}]$" "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186364534 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}]$" "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186364535 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186364536 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186364537 :question/worked-solution "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.  \n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}24 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 21\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}24-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 21-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-45\\lambda +500 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =25,\\,20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2}.$  \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2} & =100 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes   $2$ and $\\sqrt{5}.$" "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.  \n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}24 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 21\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}24-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 21-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-45\\lambda +500 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =25,\\,20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2}.$  \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2} & =100 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes   $2$ and $\\sqrt{5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186364544 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186364546 :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and\t$P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ 