end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -15\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$"] [:db/cas 17592186364551 :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-15.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=-15,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-15)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-(-15)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}12 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-15)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-15.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=-15,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-15)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-(-15)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-15)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186364553 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186364554 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186364555 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186364556 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186364557 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186364558 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$"] [:db/cas 17592186364558 :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186364560 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186364561 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186364562 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186364563 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}"]]
